<?xml version='1.0' encoding='UTF-8'?>
<!DOCTYPE sailpoint PUBLIC "sailpoint.dtd" "sailpoint.dtd">
<sailpoint>

  <!--
    Runs the logger reconciler in every IIQ JVM.

    hosts="global" is the whole trick: IIQ starts one instance of this service
    on every host in the deployment, so each JVM applies the shared
    configuration to its own log4j2 runtime. That is how a change made in the
    UI on one host reaches the others without any host-to-host communication,
    shared filesystem, or OS-specific remote execution - which in turn is why
    a mixed Windows / Linux / macOS / container cluster needs no special
    handling.

    The pluginName attribute is required, not decorative. Without it IIQ's
    Servicer resolves the executor through the webapp classloader, which
    cannot see classes inside a plugin jar, and every host logs
    "Unable to install service ... ClassNotFoundException" on startup.
    With it, the Servicer loads the class from the plugin's own classloader.

    interval=60 is the propagation delay for other hosts. The host that serves
    the UI click applies immediately, so this only governs the rest of the
    cluster. Dropping it to 15-30 makes changes feel instant everywhere at the
    cost of one small SELECT per host per tick; raising it to 300 is fine if
    you do not mind waiting five minutes for a change to land cluster-wide.

    Auto-imported on plugin install via the import/install/ convention.
  -->
  <ServiceDefinition name="TurnOnLoggersSync"
                     interval="60"
                     executor="com.example.turnonloggers.service.LoggerSyncService"
                     hosts="global">
    <Description>Applies the Logger Control plugin's logger level overrides to this host's log4j2 runtime, and reports back what this host actually has running.</Description>
    <Attributes>
      <Map>
        <entry key="pluginName" value="TurnOnLoggers"/>
      </Map>
    </Attributes>
  </ServiceDefinition>

</sailpoint>
